encyclopedia :splint do
  name "Splint"
  aka "brace", "door frame"
  kind :technology
  subkind :infrastructure
  status :complete
  log "2026-08-31 — Renamed Displaced-Door Brace to Splint; the working metaphor already describes how the frame holds mismatched geometry and supplies an ordinary term with a stable shifted meaning."
  log "2026-09-23 — Led with the singing frame and the people who live by, cheat and argue with its tone; reduced certification to one sentence."
  topics :"fluid-reality", :danger, :rebuilding, :transport, :subject_bloom
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:displacement_zone] }
  summary "A splint is the displacement country's essential furniture: a sprung ringglass frame wedged into a doorway that has opened somewhere wrong, holding the two mismatched edges apart and honest while people and furniture move through. Cordon services stage them like fire equipment."
  function "Holds the mismatched edges of a displaced doorway stable and passable during crossings"
  classes "The standard household splint, the freight pattern with roller sills, and the cordon services' rapid frame that assembles under a rescue count"
  capacity "A set splint passes walking traffic through one doorway; the freight pattern takes a handcart, and everything larger waits for the surveyors"
  operating_limit "A splint holds a settled mismatch and reports an unsettling one — the frame's tone climbs as the edges work — and splint discipline clears the door at the second tone"

  descriptive_identity(
    appearance: "A doorframe-sized rectangle of sprung ringglass members with broad shoe " \
                "plates, painted cordon orange, its corners carrying the tuning studs that " \
                "give a set splint its standing tone. Folded, it carries like a stretcher; " \
                "set, it looks like a doorway wearing a splint.",
    working: "The crew wedges the shoes against both edges of the mismatch, springs the " \
             "members to tension, and listens: a set splint hums low and steady while the " \
             "doorway holds its present geometry.",
    risks: "A seam beginning to move climbs the splint's tone in steps. At the first step " \
           "freight turns back; at the second everyone leaves, and the people who died in " \
           "splinted doorways mostly died arguing with the second step."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    In the displacement country, a doorway is an opinion the building may revise. Xyloathax's shifted quarters, the galleries around Gray Line and every district that runs #{encyclopedia_ref :thresholds, "threshold law"} have doors that open onto rooms which arrived from somewhere else. The two edges of such an opening rarely agree: floor heights offset by a hand, walls meeting at a lie of an angle, the seam between them grinding like pack ice. A splint makes the doorway crossable. The sprung ringglass frame goes into the opening, shoe plates wedged to both edges, members tensioned until the splint takes the mismatch into its own body and the doorway holds one shape long enough to use.

    Then it sings. The tuning studs give a set splint a standing tone, a low hum that tells everyone in earshot what the seam is doing. Steady means settled. A climbing tone means the geometries are working against each other. The first step up is a warning; the second clears the door, and splint country obeys it with the reflex #{ref :mareth, "Mareth"} gives its horn. People who grew up there hear a splint the way sailors hear weather, and a child can tell a first-step hum from across a market. Splint custom treats the hum as public, the way mane crews treat their smoke: anyone passing a splinted door may stop and listen, and polite people stop even when they are late.

    Households in displacement quarters keep a splint folded by the door as other households keep a fire blanket. Families who inherit a relocated room move its furniture through on the freight pattern, whose roller sills take a handcart and spare them from rationing their goods to what fits a crawl. Cordon services stage the rapid pattern at their posts, drill its assembly to a spoken count, and race each other at festivals.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :operations, heading: "Arguing With the Second Tone"
    A cordon crew sets a splint shoes-first to the true edges, springs it to the count, checks the tone against the crew's fork, and chalks the set time on the lintel as the doorway's certification. That chalk mark is the whole of the paperwork.

    The trouble lives in the gap between the first step and the second. A mover with half a room's furniture on the far side, a landlord who wants the tenants out before the room finishes its move, a family going back for the one box that matters: all of them have reasons to cross a first-tone door, and every cordon service remembers the years people argued with the second tone. Some movers file down the tuning studs so a splint hums lower and warns later. Cordon crews check for bright metal on the studs of any splint they did not set themselves, and a mover caught with filed studs loses the job and, in some districts, the right to work a splinted door again.

    When a door finishes its move, the splint stays behind in the wreck of it. Cordon services retire these frames and sell them to the survey offices, who read the deformation in the sprung members the way a crash crew reads a flight recorder. The bends map the seam's last movements, and the surveyors buy every one the services will release.
  PROSE

  cue "An orange frame stands in the doorway humming one low note, and a porter with a crate on her shoulder slows, tilts her head toward it, and walks through."
  cue "The hum steps up one clear notch, and the corridor empties away from the door before the man mid-sentence beside it has finished speaking."
  affordance "A set splint turns a mismatched doorway into passage, and its tone gives everyone in earshot a live reading on a seam that instruments would otherwise need a survey crew to watch."
  pressure "Movers with half a room on the far side cross first-tone doors, landlords push tenants through them, and some crews file the tuning studs so the splint warns later than it should."
  variation "Households keep the standard splint folded by the door like a fire blanket; cordon services drill the rapid pattern to a spoken count and time each other at festivals."
  variation "The freight pattern sits wider and lower on roller sills and hums a full tone deeper than the household splint, so crews learn two sets of warning steps."
end
